CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: mechanism
SCALE_REALISM: "Not yet shown at realistic workload. Evidence is L0 synthetic: N=2000 Zipf short docs, one planted clean answer, ~30 distractors, controlled template length/fraction, standard BM25 only. Missing real analyzer/tokenization effects, shard/index statistics, field weighting, boilerplate extraction, spam/duplicate handling, real SEO/EDGAR/Wikipedia corpora, real query set, dense retriever comparison, and downstream RAG accuracy. Reproducibility is plausible from packet paths and pre-registration, but I did not see an executed independent rerun in the packet."
RATIONALE: "YELLOW because the observed curve is supported in the packet, but the systems content is mostly a composition of already-shipping BM25 mechanisms rather than a systems design. Lucene/OpenSearch/Elasticsearch already implement the exact primitives needed for the claimed crossover: query-term IDF, TF saturation, avg document length, and b-controlled length normalization. The packet’s own mechanism table admits qterm_idf is flat across L>0, so the L-dependent recovery is length-normalization dominated; that weakens the literal claim that L-driven recovery comes from both length normalization and IDF collapse. The exact clean-answer-rank inverted-U under query-term-bearing distractor boilerplate was not found as a named shipping feature in the searched systems, but the mechanism is predictable from BM25 scoring once distractors receive query terms and then become very long. The artifact is a useful empirical stress test candidate for L1, not enough for GREEN as a systems insight/design at realistic scale."
